course: reflection
lesson: 6
title: Bitten, lost, hurt: when fear stays after the danger
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After something scary, the danger is over in minutes, but the fear can stay for weeks: the brain has learned "this can hurt" and keeps the alarm switched on.
explain: Ben reached over a fence to stroke the neighbour's dog and it bit his hand; he needed three stitches, and in bed he kept seeing its teeth: "It just went for me. Out of nowhere. What if it gets out?"
explain: For weeks he crossed the road even for tiny dogs; the dog had not hated him, it was scared or guarding its garden, and he had reached in without asking.
explain: What slowly turned the alarm down was not his cousin chasing him with a dog "to get him over it", but learning the dog rules (ask the owner, let it sniff first) and meeting one calm, friendly dog slowly at his aunt's.
explain: That is how fear after a bad experience usually shrinks: safe, small steps teach the brain again that most of the time it is okay, while being forced or keeping it secret makes it grow.
fact: After a scary event the body can stay on alert for weeks, making a person jumpy; this is normal and usually fades.
fact: Fear after a bite or a fall is the brain learning "this can hurt"; small, safe experiences slowly teach it that most of the time it is okay.
fact: Being forced into a fear too fast, like being chased with a dog or pushed into the deep end, usually makes the fear worse.
fact: Dogs often bite because they are scared or guarding their space; asking the owner first and letting a dog sniff your hand are basic dog rules.
fact: If you get lost, a person in uniform or at a shop till is a safe adult to go to; knowing your parent's phone number by heart helps.
fact: After an accident like a bike fall, doing it again slowly and safely, such as riding on grass with a helmet, helps rebuild courage.
fact: The news shows the worst things from all over the world, which can make faraway danger feel close; limiting news and asking questions helps.
fact: A burglary is when someone breaks in and steals; it is scary because home should feel safe, and the feeling of being unsafe fades with time and safety steps like new locks.
fact: Flashbacks are scary memories that come back suddenly; if fear does not fade after a scary event, talking to someone helps.
fact: Sometimes people act scary in public because they are ill, drunk or upset; the right thing is to move away and tell a grown-up.
fact: Scary films can stick in the mind, especially at night, when a tired brain mixes up what is real and not real; seeing how the monster was made can help.
fact: Most wasps and bees only sting when they feel threatened; flapping and screaming can make them more likely to sting, and people with a serious allergy carry medicine such as an EpiPen.
fact: Telling a trusted grown-up about a fear helps more than hiding it, and "you're too old to be scared" never helps.
exercise: Ben was bitten when he reached over a fence to stroke a dog. Why did the dog bite? | It was probably scared or guarding its garden | It hated Ben personally and wanted to hurt him | All dogs bite children if they get the chance | Dogs bite people who have been bitten before
exercise: For weeks Ben crosses the road even for tiny dogs. What is happening? | His brain learned "dogs can hurt" and keeps the alarm on | He has decided to dislike all dogs just to annoy his worried parents | Tiny dogs are more dangerous than big dogs | He has forgotten what dogs look like
exercise: What helped Ben's fear of dogs shrink? | Learning dog rules and meeting a calm dog slowly | His cousin chasing him with a dog to toughen him up | Never going near any dog for the rest of his life | Being told that he was a baby for being scared
exercise: Lily got lost in a shopping centre for twenty minutes. Who is a safe adult to go to? | A person in uniform or someone at a shop till | The first stranger who offers to take her outside | Nobody; she should leave the shop and walk home | Another lost child who looks about the same age
exercise: What else helped Lily after getting lost? | Practising Mum's phone number and making a plan | Being told she was too old to be scared | Never going into any shop again | Holding Mum's hand forever and never letting go
exercise: Jack broke his arm falling off his bike downhill. What helped him ride again? | Short slow rides on grass, wearing his helmet | Friends saying "just do it, don't be a baby" | Riding fast down the same hill on day one | Selling the bike so he never had to ride again
exercise: Mia saw a news story about a war with crying children and now can't sleep. What helped her? | Dad showing her where the war was on a map and limiting the news | Watching lots more videos about the war before bed so she could understand it | Keeping her fear secret so Dad wouldn't worry | Being told that wars happen everywhere every day
exercise: Ryan's house was burgled on holiday. What is a burglary? | When someone breaks in and steals things | When a storm breaks the windows of a house | When a family moves house without telling anyone | When the police search a house carefully for clues
exercise: What helped Ryan feel safe at home again? | New locks, and making his room feel like his own again | Imagining the burglar every night so he would always be ready for him | Sleeping in the car outside the house | Moving out of the house the very next day
exercise: Amira was in a car crash, and now she jumps at loud noises. What is true? | The body can stay on alert after a scare, and it usually fades | She will jump at every loud noise for the rest of her life, whatever she does | Jumping at noises means she was badly hurt inside | It only happens to people who were driving
exercise: What helped Amira feel okay in cars again? | Talking about it, breathing exercises and short trips first | Avoiding cars completely for the rest of the year, and walking everywhere | Long drives on the motorway straight away | Never mentioning the crash to anyone
exercise: Leo was pulled under at the pool by a bigger kid messing around and panicked. What helped him? | Lessons with a kind teacher and telling the lifeguard | Being thrown straight into the deep end so he had to face his fear | Staying out of every pool for the rest of his life | Pulling the bigger kid under to get even
exercise: Sophie saw a man shouting and throwing things at the bus stop. What is the right thing to do? | Move away and tell a grown-up | Go closer to find out what's wrong | Shout back at him to make him stop | Stay frozen in place until he leaves
exercise: Ethan watched a horror film too old for him at a sleepover and now fears what's under his bed. What helped him? | Telling Mum and watching how the monster was made | Watching the film again on his own to prove it's not real | Pretending he wasn't scared at all | Sleeping under the bed to check it himself
exercise: Holly's face swelled after a wasp sting and she went to hospital. What is true about wasps? | Most only sting when they feel threatened | Wasps chase people on purpose to sting them | Flapping your arms about scares wasps away fast | Every buzzing insect will sting you on sight
exercise: What did NOT help Holly around wasps? | Screaming and flapping her arms | Learning about how wasps really behave | Carrying her EpiPen with her everywhere | Staying calm when one flew nearby
test: Ben's cousin says, "I'll chase you with Rex till you're not scared, sorted." What's wrong with that? | Being forced makes fear worse; slow, safe steps help | Nothing, chasing someone with a dog is the best and fastest cure for fear | Rex might get scared, which is the only problem | It would work, but only if the dog is very big
test: Lily's big brother says, "you're nine, too old to be scared of getting lost, lol." What's true? | Getting lost is scary at any age; a plan helps | He's right; nine-year-olds never feel scared about things like that | Being scared means she'll get lost again soon | Only babies are scared when they get lost
test: Jack's mate from Down Under goes, "nah yeah, just send it down the hill again, mate." ("Send it" means go for it at full speed.) What's the better idea? | Start with short, slow rides on grass, with his helmet on | Do it straight away at full speed, to show everyone how brave he is | Never touch a bike again for the rest of his life | Ride without a helmet so he can see better
test: What would happen if Mia watched every war video she could find before bed? | Her fear would probably grow, not shrink | She would feel safe at once and sleep well | The war would end sooner because she watched | Nothing; news never affects how people feel
test: In a cartoon, a burglar comes back to the same house every single night. Is that how it usually is? | No; most burglars don't come back, and locks help | Yes; burglars always return to the same house | Yes; that's why families must move after a burglary | No, burglars only steal from shops, never houses
test: Amira's gran says, "she's still a wee bit jumpy, aye, since the crash." What does "jumpy" mean and is it normal? | Easily startled; normal after a scare, and it fades | Very happy; it's normal after any trip | Bad at sport; it means she hurt her legs in the crash | Very sleepy; it means she needs a doctor
test: A Year 6 kid says, "flapping your arms scares wasps off, everyone knows that." Is he right? | No; flapping can make a wasp feel threatened and sting | Yes; wasps are always frightened of moving arms | Yes, but only if you scream at the same time | No, because wasps can't see arms at all
test: Sophie's cousin from Manchester: "some fella was proper kickin' off at the bus stop." ("Kicking off" means getting angry and loud.) What should a kid do? | Move away, steer clear and tell a grown-up | Go closer and film it on a phone | Shout at him to calm down right now | Keep it secret so nobody gets in trouble
test: Ethan's mate says, "that film's real, they found the monster in my uncle's town, no cap." What's true? | It's a made-up film with special effects, not real | It's real, because his uncle saw it himself | It's real, but the monster only comes out at night | Nobody can know if a film is real or not
test: Leo's mate says, "he ducked you as a joke, can't you take a joke?" ("Ducked" means pushed under the water.) What's the truth? | Ducking someone isn't funny; telling the lifeguard is right | It's a fair joke, because pools are for messing around | Leo should duck him back to get even | Lifeguards don't care about things like that
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the persistence of fear subsequent to a frightening event? | The body may remain on alert for a period which commonly diminishes | Fear invariably ceases upon the conclusion of the danger | Fear persists only in persons of a nervous disposition | Fear subsequent to an event invariably endures for life
exam: Identify the method most consistent with the established facts for the reduction of fear acquired through a bite or a fall. | Gradual and safe renewed experience of the feared activity | Sudden and compulsory exposure to the greatest danger | Total and permanent avoidance of the feared activity | Ridicule of the frightened person by his companions
exam: It is given that a child has become separated from a parent in a large shop. Determine the most appropriate course of action. | Approach a uniformed staff member and state the parent's number | Depart the premises alone and proceed homeward on foot | Accept the first offer of assistance from any passing stranger | Remain concealed until the premises close for the night
exam: Ascertain which of the following is consistent with the facts concerning the conduct of wasps. | Wasps commonly sting when they perceive themselves to be threatened | Wasps pursue human beings deliberately in order to sting them | Wasps are dispersed effectively by vigorous flapping of the arms | Wasps are incapable of stinging any person more than once
